bug_descriptions |bug_id, line_num, content|
    seed_typed_f64_option_some_heap_allocates_per_some_never_released_2026-11-27, 0, "Owner decision 2026-11-27: typed f64?/Option<f64> = tag plus the exact 64-bit payload inline, no heap allocation. Seed today: every explicit Some(x) is rt_enum_new (a 32-byte RuntimeEnum registered through track_transient_heap, codegen/instr/result.rs create_enum_value; LLVM functions.rs OptionSome likewise) and the flat form (return x into an f64? slot) heap-boxes a HeapFloat (rt_value_float). Measured aarch64, seeds e2479b4127a0a3a9 (base) and the exactness fix of seed_jit_explicit_f64_option_some_typed_readers_lose_low_mantissa_bits_2026-11-27: a 4e7 construct + ?? + unwrap loop holds 1.94 GB max RSS (about 48 B per Some), identical on both, never released. An inline form needs a two-word value (tag + payload; no 64-bit NaN-box can carry every f64 plus None) through MIR locals, struct fields, [f64?] element storage, params and the return ABI in cranelift and LLVM and the C runtime. The pure-Simple compiler (src/compiler/50.mir switch_operators_calls.spl bitcast_float_enum_payload / ensure_option_handle) also uses the heap rt_enum_new handle for every Optional. Not attempted in the exactness lane."

